      "note_id": "notes/references/2026-03-10-go-tools.md",
      "ordinal": 0,
      "body": "# Go Tools"
    },
    {
      "note_id": "notes/references/2026-03-10-go-tools.md",
      "ordinal": 1,
      "body": "Sea Noise uses Go tools to build a small static runtime. Go Tools keeps the vault boundary explicit. Amber Engine documents the example boundary."
    },
    {
      "note_id": "notes/references/2026-03-11-markdown-vault.md",
      "ordinal": 0,
      "body": "# Markdown Vault"
    },
    {
      "note_id": "notes/references/2026-03-11-markdown-vault.md",
      "ordinal": 1,
      "body": "The Markdown Vault stays outside the binary. Go Tools read it through a narrow interface. Amber Engine records the source boundary."
    },
    {
      "note_id": "notes/references/2026-03-12-context-assembly.md",
      "ordinal": 0,
      "body": "# Context Assembly"
    },
    {
      "note_id": "notes/references/2026-03-12-context-assembly.md",
      "ordinal": 1,
      "body": "Context Assembly combines selected Markdown notes without taking ownership of the source vault. Amber Engine keeps the assembly deterministic."
    },
    {
      "note_id": "notes/references/2026-03-13-retrieval-index.md",
      "ordinal": 0,
      "body": "# Retrieval Index"
    },
    {
      "note_id": "notes/references/2026-03-13-retrieval-index.md",
      "ordinal": 1,
      "body": "The Retrieval Index is derived state. Go Tools can rebuild it when the source vault changes. Amber Engine makes the rebuild observable."
    },
    {
      "note_id": "notes/references/2026-03-14-clean-checks.md",
      "ordinal": 0,
      "body": "# Clean Checks"
    },
    {
      "note_id": "notes/references/2026-03-14-clean-checks.md",
      "ordinal": 1,
      "body": "Clean Checks make a public release reproducible from a fresh checkout. Amber Engine is the neutral entity used by the profile parity test."
    },
    {
      "note_id": "notes/references/2026-04-12-example-workflow.md",
      "ordinal": 0,
      "body": "# Example Workflow"
    },
    {
      "note_id": "notes/references/2026-04-12-example-workflow.md",
      "ordinal": 1,
      "body": "## Transcript"
    },
    {
      "note_id": "notes/references/2026-04-12-example-workflow.md",
      "ordinal": 2,
      "body": "This synthetic transcript explains how a small command can keep a Markdown vault useful. It separates configuration from execution and keeps the external vault as the source of truth. A simple workflow favors clear boundaries, repeatable checks, and explicit recovery when a command fails. The example is intentionally fictional and exists only to exercise transcript enrichment."
    },
    {
      "note_id": "notes/references/2026-04-12-example-workflow.md",
      "ordinal": 3,
      "body": "The speaker compares a source document with derived indexes and asks where ownership should live. A source vault can remain an ordinary directory while a runtime builds temporary views, search indexes, and summaries beside it. Each derived artifact should be rebuildable, and each mutating command should say which files it writes. This makes a first run understandable and makes a failed run recoverable."
    },
    {
      "note_id": "notes/references/2026-04-12-example-workflow.md",
      "ordinal": 4,
      "body": "The transcript also describes small operational habits: keep configuration near the command, prefer boring standard formats, make dry runs available for destructive work, and record enough context to explain a result later. These are general engineering examples rather than claims about a real product or person. The test fixture keeps the language generic so its output can be published safely."
    }
